   "type": "mcq",
   "marks": 2,
   "difficulty": "medium",
   "year": 2003,
   "text": "Let $G = ( V, E )$ be a directed graph with vertices. A path from $v _ { i }$ to $v _ { j }$ in $G$ is a sequence of vertices ( $v _ { i }, v _ { i + 1 }, \\ldots, v _ { j } )$ such that $( v _ { k }, v _ { k + 1 } ) \\in E$ for all in through $j - 1.$ A simple path is a path in which no vertex appears more than once. Let be an $n \\times n$ array initialized as follows: $A [ j, k ] = \\left\\{ \\begin{array}{l l} 1, & \\text {if} (j, k) \\in E \\\\ 0, & \\text {otherwise} \\end{array} \\right.$ Consider the following algorithm: Which of the following statements is necessarily true for all and after termination of the above algorithm?",
   "options": [
    "$A [ j , k ] \\leq n$",
    "If $A [ j , j ] \\geq n - 1$ then has a Hamiltonian cycle",
    "If there exists a path from to $k , A [ j , k ]$ contains the longest path length from to",
    "If there exists a path from to , every simple path from to contains at most $A [ j , k ]$ edges"
   ],
   "answer": [
    3
   ],
   "code_blocks": [
    {
     "lang": "",
     "code": "for i=1 to n\n    for j=1 to n\n        for k=1 to n\n            A[j,k] = max(A[j,k], A[j,i] + A[i,k]);"
    }
   ],
